From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Subject: x11: don't stack a window against the focus when no window has it

-orderwindow::: puts a window ordered in front (NSWindowAbove, no other
window) just below the window that has the input focus.  When no window
has it, XGetInputFocus answers None or PointerRoot, and the window was
stacked against PointerRoot (1), so the X server answered BadWindow.
This happens when the focused window closes and the focus reverts to the
pointer root, as with a window manager that leaves it there.  Such a
window is now stacked above the others, as when XGetInputFocus fails.

Tests/x11/windowterm.m (in the next patch) shows it: under its test window
manager each window it opens after closing one gave BadWindow on
ConfigureWindow, 3 in 3; with this change none.

AI assistance: this change and this description were written with Claude
(Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Carried from the Adwaita libs-back series, 0004a (cfaded2), without its ChangeLog entry.

diff --git a/Source/x11/XGServerWindow.m b/Source/x11/XGServerWindow.m
index a442fb1..25c774d 100644
--- a/Source/x11/XGServerWindow.m
+++ b/Source/x11/XGServerWindow.m
@@ -3323,7 +3323,9 @@ swapColors(unsigned char *image_data, NSBitmapImageRep *rep)
       int revert, status;
       status = XGetInputFocus(dpy, &keywin, &revert);
       other = NULL;
-      if (status == True)
+      /* With no window focused, the focus is None or PointerRoot, which are
+         no windows to stack against. */
+      if (status == True && keywin != None && keywin != PointerRoot)
 	{
 	  /* Alloc a temporary window structure */
 	  other = GSAutoreleasedBuffer(sizeof(gswindow_device_t));
